Nonspecific Presyncope / Lightheadedness
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Sensation of impending faint/blackout | Complete absence of true rotational vertigo | Normal orthostatic vital signs

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-013
Reason for Visit: Acute, recurrent episodes of near-fainting requiring urgent assessment to exclude cardiogenic syncope or acute internal hemorrhage.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate repeated episodes of lightheadedness and presyncope. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out life-threatening arrhythmias, acute gastrointestinal bleeding, or severe hypotension, preventing complete cardiovascular collapse prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Every time I try to walk to the bathroom, my vision tunnels out, my ears ring, and I feel like I am going to pass out.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden, repeated sensation of impending faintness without actual loss of consciousness. This is accompanied by generalized weakness and "jelly legs." The patient describes transient visual blurring, tunnel vision, and muffled hearing or tinnitus just before the near-faint. There is concurrent nausea, diaphoresis, skin pallor, and palpitations. The patient notes absolute improvement of symptoms upon lying supine and explicit provocation by prolonged standing or sudden postural changes.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound; unable to ambulate safely due to presyncope.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate, extreme fall risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: Supine 118/72 mmHg, Standing 108/68 mmHg | HR: Supine 78 bpm, Standing 96 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 66 Kg
General: Patient appears pale and mildly diaphoretic when upright, but color returns rapidly when supine.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a regular rhythm with no murmurs. Cardiovascular history notes a historical pattern of elevated clinic blood pressure with normal home blood pressure and a distinct lack of target organ damage. Respiratory exam is clear. Abdomen is soft, non-tender, with no palpable masses or signs of acute gastrointestinal hemorrhage (negative rectal exam/melena history). Neurological examination is globally intact without focal signs.
Environmental Observations: High ambient room temperature; limited access to oral fluids near the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient recently started a new alpha-blocker for benign prostatic hyperplasia.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Nonspecific Presyncope / Lightheadedness - Status: Acute exacerbation secondary to orthostasis and mild vasodilation.
Differentials:

Cardiogenic Syncope (Arrhythmia) - Ruled out clinically due to the strict positional nature of the symptoms, normal resting heart rate, and lack of symptoms while supine.

Acute Gastrointestinal Bleed - Ruled out due to stable supine hemodynamics, absence of melena/hematemesis, and normal abdominal exam.

Acute Ischemic Stroke (Posterior Circulation) - Ruled out due to the absence of persistent ataxia, dysarthria, diplopia, or focal weakness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Fall risk).

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's condition necessitated immediate after-hours home assessment due to repetitive, severe presyncopal episodes preventing safe ambulation. Telephone triage appropriately identified the high risk for cardiogenic syncope or acute hemorrhagic shock. Detailed orthostatic vital signs and a comprehensive physical exam confirmed positional cardiovascular reactivity without underlying structural cardiac failure, ischemic neurology, or hemorrhagic compromise. This urgent evaluation secured the patient's immediate environmental safety and definitively prevented an unnecessary trauma or emergency department admission by diagnosing a benign, positionally triggered vasomotor response.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed patient on physical counter-pressure maneuvers. The expected investigation results include a normal electrocardiogram and minor drops in orthostatic blood pressure not fulfilling strict criteria for orthostatic hypotension, which would assist in confirming the diagnosis via routine GP follow-up.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain cerebral perfusion, perform ankle pumps and calf squeezes for thirty seconds before transitioning from lying to sitting; physically engaging the muscle pump will immediately increase venous return and prevent sudden blood pressure drops.
Home-Based Services: None indicated.
DME/Safety: Advised strict use of a bedside commode tonight to eliminate walking distances during vulnerable periods.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours for a formal medication review and 12-lead ECG.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient experiences a complete loss of consciousness, develops severe chest pain, or exhibits sudden unilateral weakness or difficulty speaking.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal resting and ambulatory electrocardiograms.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves intermittent positional instability that resolves rapidly with postural correction and adequate hydration.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if you actually faint and hit your head, or if your heart starts racing uncontrollably while you are lying perfectly still.

